WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.815
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level five.

2
00:00:06.115 --> 00:00:09.872
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:10.172 --> 00:00:16.540
Unit nine: Rhetorical Analysis. Today you will link ideas with not only… but also, both… and, and whether… or.

4
00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:19.423
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.367 --> 00:00:23.762
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.617 --> 00:00:26.071
Rhetorical strategy.

7
00:00:27.083 --> 00:00:31.126
Rhetorical strategy means a planned way of using language to persuade.

8
00:00:31.717 --> 00:00:34.517
Storytelling is a common rhetorical strategy.

9
00:00:35.117 --> 00:00:38.315
Your turn. Say: Rhetorical strategy.

10
00:00:41.167 --> 00:00:42.992
Rhetorical strategy.

11
00:00:43.750 --> 00:00:45.250
Audience awareness.

12
00:00:46.250 --> 00:00:50.618
Audience awareness means understanding who is reading or listening and what they need.

13
00:00:51.217 --> 00:00:54.319
Audience awareness shapes every word choice.

14
00:00:54.917 --> 00:00:57.941
Your turn. Say: Audience awareness.

15
00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:02.324
Audience awareness.

16
00:01:03.083 --> 00:01:04.491
Persuasive appeal.

17
00:01:05.483 --> 00:01:10.165
Persuasive appeal means a way of winning agreement through character, emotion or logic.

18
00:01:10.750 --> 00:01:13.226
Each persuasive appeal works differently.

19
00:01:13.817 --> 00:01:16.933
Your turn. Say: Persuasive appeal.

20
00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:21.300
Persuasive appeal.

21
00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:23.039
Ethos.

22
00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:27.791
Ethos means an appeal based on the speaker's character and trustworthiness.

23
00:01:28.383 --> 00:01:30.998
A doctor's ethos makes advice credible.

24
00:01:31.583 --> 00:01:34.212
Your turn. Say: Ethos.

25
00:01:37.067 --> 00:01:37.998
Ethos.

26
00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:39.867
Pathos.

27
00:01:40.883 --> 00:01:44.357
Pathos means an appeal that stirs the audience's emotions.

28
00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:47.135
The photograph relied on pathos.

29
00:01:47.750 --> 00:01:50.286
Your turn. Say: Pathos.

30
00:01:53.133 --> 00:01:54.227
Pathos.

31
00:01:54.983 --> 00:01:55.973
Logos.

32
00:01:56.983 --> 00:02:00.492
Logos means an appeal based on logic, facts and reasoning.

33
00:02:01.083 --> 00:02:03.872
Logos uses statistics and clear steps.

34
00:02:04.483 --> 00:02:07.147
Your turn. Say: Logos.

35
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:11.152
Logos.

36
00:02:11.917 --> 00:02:13.405
Rhetorical situation.

37
00:02:14.417 --> 00:02:18.912
Rhetorical situation means the context of speaker, audience, purpose and occasion.

38
00:02:19.517 --> 00:02:22.120
The rhetorical situation determines the tone.

39
00:02:22.717 --> 00:02:25.671
Your turn. Say: Rhetorical situation.

40
00:02:28.533 --> 00:02:30.498
Rhetorical situation.

41
00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:32.588
Argument structure.

42
00:02:33.583 --> 00:02:37.661
Argument structure means the order in which claims and evidence are arranged.

43
00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:40.586
A clear argument structure guides the reader.

44
00:02:41.183 --> 00:02:44.196
Your turn. Say: Argument structure.

45
00:02:47.033 --> 00:02:48.452
Argument structure.

46
00:02:49.267 --> 00:02:52.720
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:53.583 --> 00:02:59.812
Visal, an analysis says 'The speech appeals not only to logic but also to emotion.' What does the pair do?

48
00:03:00.917 --> 00:03:13.521
Correlative conjunctions join two parallel parts: not only… but also, both… and, whether… or. They show balance and add emphasis. Parallel parts must have the same grammar.

49
00:03:14.617 --> 00:03:16.303
What does 'same grammar' mean?

50
00:03:17.417 --> 00:03:33.353
If the first part is a noun phrase, so is the second: both ethos and pathos. Not 'both ethos and appealing to emotion'. Whether she informs or entertains, she wins attention.

51
00:03:34.450 --> 00:03:35.753
And with inversion?

52
00:03:36.850 --> 00:03:49.301
If not only begins the sentence, invert: Not only does the speech use logos, but it also builds trust. This is formal and gives strong emphasis.

53
00:03:50.467 --> 00:03:51.758
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:52.617 --> 00:03:58.053
Grammar focus: Correlatives: not only… but also and both… and and whether… or.

55
00:03:58.353 --> 00:03:59.528
Look at the pattern.

56
00:04:00.217 --> 00:04:03.586
The speech appeals not only to logic but also to emotion.

57
00:04:04.483 --> 00:04:06.727
She uses both ethos and pathos.

58
00:04:07.617 --> 00:04:11.543
Not only does the speaker use logos, but she also builds trust.

59
00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:22.504
Join equal parts with not only… but also, both… and, whether… or. Keep the grammar parallel. After Not only at the start of a sentence, invert the subject and auxiliary.

60
00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:27.458
Be careful! Don't say: Not only she wrote, but she also taught.

61
00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:33.187
Say: Not only did she write, but she also taught.

62
00:04:33.487 --> 00:04:35.708
Invert the subject after Not only.

63
00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:39.210
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:41.767
Three Ways to Persuade.

65
00:04:42.067 --> 00:04:45.924
Rhetorical analysis asks how a text persuades its audience.

66
00:04:46.933 --> 00:04:51.696
A strong speaker uses both ethos and logos, and the best also add pathos.

67
00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:58.682
Not only does audience awareness shape the tone, but it also decides which persuasive appeal comes first.

68
00:04:59.667 --> 00:05:05.672
Whether the rhetorical situation is a courtroom or a rally, the speaker must choose a clear argument structure.

69
00:05:06.667 --> 00:05:12.126
A rhetorical strategy is effective not only when it is logical but also when it fits the audience.

70
00:05:13.233 --> 00:05:17.212
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:18.050 --> 00:05:20.618
She uses blank logos and pathos.

72
00:05:20.918 --> 00:05:24.119
A, both. B, either. C, neither.

73
00:05:27.667 --> 00:05:29.248
The answer is A: both.

74
00:05:30.383 --> 00:05:32.615
An appeal based on emotion is blank.

75
00:05:32.915 --> 00:05:36.801
A, ethos. B, pathos. C, logos.

76
00:05:40.367 --> 00:05:42.401
The answer is B: pathos.

77
00:05:43.550 --> 00:05:45.178
Which sentence is correct?

78
00:05:45.478 --> 00:05:54.008
A, Not only she is clear, but she is kind.. B, Not only is she clear, but she is also kind.. C, Not only she clear is, but also kind..

79
00:05:57.567 --> 00:06:01.296
The answer is B: Not only is she clear, but she is also kind..

80
00:06:02.450 --> 00:06:05.262
An appeal based on logic and facts is blank.

81
00:06:05.562 --> 00:06:09.204
A, ethos. B, pathos. C, logos.

82
00:06:12.767 --> 00:06:14.801
The answer is C: logos.

83
00:06:15.950 --> 00:06:19.296
According to the reading, what must a speaker choose?

84
00:06:19.596 --> 00:06:24.887
A, a clear argument structure. B, a longer title. C, a quiet room.

85
00:06:28.433 --> 00:06:31.547
The answer is A: a clear argument structure.

86
00:06:32.733 --> 00:06:35.943
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:36.783 --> 00:06:42.440
Which persuasive appeal — ethos, pathos, or logos — do you find most convincing, and why?

88
00:06:51.250 --> 00:06:52.657
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:52.957 --> 00:07:14.806
I find logos the most convincing, because facts and clear reasoning can be checked. However, I think the best arguments use all three. Ethos makes me trust the speaker, pathos makes me care, and logos shows that the claim is sound. Not only does a balanced argument persuade, but it also respects the audience.

90
00:07:15.106 --> 00:07:16.909
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:17.817 --> 00:07:24.254
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and correlatives: not only… but also and both… and and whether… or.

92
00:07:24.554 --> 00:07:29.133
Today's value is reasoned judgment. Weigh the evidence before you decide what to believe.

93
00:07:29.433 --> 00:07:31.714
See you in Unit ten. Goodbye!

94
00:07:32.014 --> 00:07:35.270
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

